// Wonder Lab earth-science data. Generated by tools/build_extras.py
// Written and fact-checked in review passes; do not hand-edit.
// 97 facts across 8 sections.
const EARTH = [{"id":"water-1","section":"water","cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"The deepest measured spot in the ocean is the Challenger Deep, at the southern end of the Mariana Trench: 35,876 feet (10,935 m) below the surface. Drop Mount Everest into it and there would still be more than a mile of water over the summit.","more":"That figure comes from a careful 2020 sonar survey and is good to about 20 feet either way. It is a depth someone measured, not a depth anyone calculated."},{"id":"water-10","section":"water","cat":"tryit","kind":"found","text":"A cold glass does not leak. The water on the outside came out of the air in the room. Air carries invisible water, and chilling it makes the air let go. That single step, warm damp air meeting something cold, is how clouds and rain get made.","tryit":"Fill a glass with ice water and leave it a few minutes. Wipe the outside completely dry with your sleeve, then watch it go wet again. Nothing came through the glass. The room handed it over."},{"id":"water-11","section":"water","cat":"build","kind":"worked","text":"The depth of a trench is measured. How it got there is not. Nobody has watched a trench form. The deepest ones are worked out to be places where one slab of sea floor bends down under another, because the earthquakes beneath them line up on a slanting sheet running down and away.","more":"The measured part is where each quake happened and how deep. The bending slab is the explanation that fits the pattern, and an explanation is a different kind of thing from a depth reading."},{"id":"water-12","section":"water","cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"In Genesis, water is there before anything has a shape. On day two the waters are divided, water below from water above. On day three the water below is gathered into one place so that dry land can appear. The written account makes water the setting everything else gets arranged in.","more":"This one is a record, not a reading. It is worth noticing that the ocean really is one connected body of water rather than several, which is why a bottle dropped off Japan can wash up in California."},{"id":"water-2","section":"water","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"At the bottom of the Challenger Deep the water presses in at over a thousand times the pressure at the surface, roughly eight tons on every square inch. Anything with air inside it, including a person, would be crushed flat. People have still been down there, inside thick steel spheres.","more":"Jacques Piccard and Don Walsh reached the bottom in 1960. James Cameron went alone in 2012, and Victor Vescovo has been down repeatedly since 2019."},{"id":"water-3","section":"water","cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"Of all the water on Earth, 96.5 percent is salt water sitting in the oceans. Of the small slice that is fresh, most is not available at all: nearly 69 percent of it is locked up as ice, and about 30 percent is underground. Lakes and rivers get the leftovers.","more":"Add every lake, river, swamp and puddle together and you have well under one percent of the world's fresh water, which is under three percent of its water in total."},{"id":"water-4","section":"water","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Every river on Earth put together holds about one ten-thousandth of one percent of the planet's water. Nearly all the water people drink, wash in and grow food with comes from that sliver. It only works because rain keeps refilling it faster than we empty it.","more":"A river is less a store of water than a queue of it. What is in the Mississippi today is not what was in it last month."},{"id":"water-5","section":"water","cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"The Amazon pours roughly 7.6 million cubic feet (215,000 cubic meters) of fresh water into the Atlantic every second. That is about five times what the Congo delivers and twelve times the Mississippi, and it works out to something like a fifth of all the river water reaching the oceans.","more":"The outflow is so large that the sea off the mouth of the Amazon is noticeably less salty, and sailors used to refill their fresh water barrels while still out of sight of land."},{"id":"water-6","section":"water","cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"Tides in the Bay of Fundy in Canada rise and fall about 52 feet (16 m) twice a day. Boats afloat at breakfast sit on mud by lunch. Around 100 billion tons of water move in and out each cycle, more than every river in the world carries in that same stretch of time.","more":"The highest level ever recorded there was 71 feet (21.6 m), in October 1869, when a storm surge happened to land on top of an already big tide."},{"id":"water-7","section":"water","cat":"travel","kind":"found","text":"High tide arrives about 50 minutes later each day. So does the moon. That is not a coincidence and it is not a theory anyone has to take on trust: tide tables are printed a year in advance, and boats stake their keels on them being right.","more":"The moon takes 24 hours and 50 minutes to come back around to the same place in your sky, which is why the whole tidal pattern slides later at exactly that rate."},{"id":"water-8","section":"water","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"The ocean covers about 70 percent of the planet, and only around 29 percent of the sea floor has been mapped in detail with modern sonar. The moon has been mapped in finer detail than the bottom of our own sea.","more":"Mapping is not the same as seeing. Less than one thousandth of one percent of the deep sea floor has ever actually been looked at by a camera or a person, an area about the size of Rhode Island."},{"id":"water-9","section":"water","cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"Angel Falls in Venezuela drops 3,212 feet (979 m) in total, including one unbroken plunge of 2,648 feet (807 m). The water falls for so long before anything interrupts it that a good deal of it arrives at the bottom as drifting spray rather than as a river.","more":"It is named for Jimmie Angel, an American pilot who flew over the falls on 16 November 1933. The name first appeared on a Venezuelan government map in December 1939."},{"id":"ice-1","section":"ice","cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"About nine-tenths of an iceberg is underwater. Ice is roughly nine-tenths as heavy as seawater for the same amount of space, so a berg has to sink until it has shoved aside its own weight in water. Only that last tenth is left sticking up where you can see it.","more":"This is why a berg can roll over without warning. As the underwater part melts unevenly, the whole thing becomes top-heavy and flips, and there is no way to see it coming from a ship. Captains keep well clear of the sides, not just the front."},{"id":"ice-10","section":"ice","cat":"tryit","kind":"found","text":"Your breath is full of water vapour you cannot see. Push it against something colder than the air and the vapour turns straight back into liquid: fog on the glass. If the glass is below freezing, it skips being liquid altogether and goes directly to ice crystals, which is frost.","more":"The same thing is happening on the inside of a cold window in winter, in the mist that comes off a cold drink can, and in a cloud. In all four cases warm air holding invisible water meets something cold and has to let the water go.","tryit":"Breathe hard on a cold window and watch the patch appear, then shrink and vanish. On a freezing morning, do it on the outside of a car window instead and get close: the fog arrives as tiny crystals, not as droplets."},{"id":"ice-11","section":"ice","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"At the South Pole the sun rises once a year and sets once a year. It comes up around late September, then circles round and round the sky without setting for six months, and goes down around late March. One day and one night, six months each.","more":"It does not go straight up and straight down there either. It spirals: a slow climb to about 23 degrees above the horizon at midsummer, then a slow spiral back down. Sunrise at the Pole takes days."},{"id":"ice-12","section":"ice","cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Genesis day one: light, and light divided from darkness, and the two of them named Day and Night. All of that before the sun is made on day four. At the South Pole, Day and Night are six months long apiece. This is the written account, not something anyone measured.","more":"Standing at the Pole is the one place on earth where day and night are not tied to the sun coming up in the morning. They are a single long turn of light and dark, which is a strange thing to stand inside of."},{"id":"ice-2","section":"ice","cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"At the South Pole the ice is about 9,000 feet (2,700 m) thick. Nearly two miles of it. The rock underneath is close to sea level, so almost the whole of that 9,301-foot (2,835 m) elevation is frozen water standing on the ground, with the research station standing on top of the water.","more":"The depth is read by flying radar over the ice and timing how long the echo takes to come back off the rock below. Where that has been checked against actual drilled holes, it lines up."},{"id":"ice-3","section":"ice","cat":"travel","kind":"found","text":"The marker at the geographic South Pole is dug up and moved every New Year's Day. The ice it stands on, along with the station built on that ice, drifts about 33 feet (10 m) a year toward the Weddell Sea. Leave it alone and it stops marking the pole.","more":"The crew who winter over design and machine a new brass marker each year, and the old one is retired. It may be the only signpost on earth that has to keep chasing the place it names."},{"id":"ice-4","section":"ice","cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"The thickest ice measured anywhere in Antarctica is close to 3 miles (4.9 km) deep, in the Astrolabe Subglacial Basin. Across the whole continent the average is about 1.4 miles (2.2 km). Ice covers roughly 98 percent of Antarctica, so most maps of it are really maps of the lid.","more":"There are mountain ranges under there that no one has ever seen, mapped only by radar. There are lakes too, kept liquid by the pressure and the warmth of the rock, sitting under miles of ice."},{"id":"ice-5","section":"ice","cat":"size","kind":"worked","text":"If every bit of Antarctica's ice melted, sea level would go up about 190 feet (58 m). Greenland's would add roughly 24 feet (7.4 m). Nobody has measured that, obviously. Those are sums, worked out from the measured volume of the ice and the measured area of the world's oceans.","more":"Two numbers, two different kinds of claim, in one sentence. The volume of the ice is surveyed. The sea level figure is arithmetic done on that survey. Neither one is a prediction about whether it will happen."},{"id":"ice-6","section":"ice","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Floating sea ice melting does not raise sea level. It is already shoving aside exactly its own weight in water, so turning back into water changes nothing. Ice sitting on land is a completely different case, because that water is not in the ocean yet. Same substance, opposite result.","more":"Float an ice cube in a glass filled to the very brim and mark the level. When it melts, the glass still does not overflow. Now set a second cube on a saucer, let it melt, and pour that in. That one is land ice."},{"id":"ice-7","section":"ice","cat":"senses","kind":"found","text":"Snow is white and glacier ice is blue, and both are made of the identical clear stuff. Snow is packed with tiny surfaces that bounce every colour of light straight back out at you. Thick solid ice lets light travel far enough inside to soak up the reds, and blue is what comes back.","more":"You can watch the effect turn on. A shallow scoop of snow is white; a deep hole punched into a glacier goes blue at the bottom, because the light down there has had to travel further through solid ice to get back to your eye."},{"id":"ice-8","section":"ice","cat":"speed","kind":"found","text":"Jakobshavn Glacier in Greenland moves more than 150 feet (45 m) a day in summer. That is about an inch and a half every minute, which means a stake driven into the ice shifts several feet while you eat lunch. It is among the fastest-moving glaciers measured anywhere.","more":"It moves that fast because meltwater gets down to the bottom and the whole slab slides on it, rather than just creeping. Most glaciers are far lazier, moving a few inches a day, which is where the insult glacial pace came from."},{"id":"ice-9","section":"ice","cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"Permafrost is not ice. It is ground, any ground, that has stayed at or below 32 F (0 C) for at least two years running. About a quarter of the land in the northern half of the world qualifies, and in places it reaches down more than 4,900 feet (1,500 m).","more":"The top layer thaws every summer and refreezes every winter, so the trees, moss and mosquitoes live on a thin lid over frozen ground. Build a heated house on it without lifting the house off the ground first and the heat thaws the lid, and the house sinks."},{"id":"weather-1","section":"weather","cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"A lightning bolt heats the air in its path to about 50,000 F (27,800 C). The surface of the sun is around 10,000 F (5,500 C). So the air inside a lightning channel is roughly five times hotter than the surface of the sun, for about a ten-thousandth of a second.","more":"It is not that the bolt is hot to begin with. Lightning is a huge current of electricity forcing its way through air, which is a terrible conductor. The air fights it, and that resistance is what dumps the heat, the same way a thin wire glows orange inside a toaster."},{"id":"weather-10","section":"weather","cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"An ordinary puffy cumulus cloud carries around 1.1 million pounds (500,000 kg) of water. That is a loaded jumbo jet, parked in the sky. It stays up because the water is chopped into droplets far too small to fall quickly, and the air underneath the cloud is denser than the cloud is.","more":"The droplets are falling, just extremely slowly, and rising air underneath keeps shoving them back up. Rain starts when droplets bump into each other and merge until they are heavy enough to win the argument."},{"id":"weather-11","section":"weather","cat":"speed","kind":"found","text":"The fastest wind ever measured in a tornado was read by a radar mounted on a truck near Bridge Creek, Oklahoma on 3 May 1999. The first figure was about 301 mph (484 km/h). A careful re-check in 2021 raised it to 321 mph (517 km/h).","more":"The radar was looking at wind a few hundred feet up, not at ground level. No weather instrument sitting on the ground survives a tornado core, which is why tornado strength is normally rated afterwards, from what the damage looks like."},{"id":"weather-12","section":"weather","cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"On day two the waters were divided, water below from water above. The water above is overhead right now: a million pounds of it riding inside one ordinary afternoon cloud.","more":"Worth keeping the two kinds of statement apart. That a cloud holds about a million pounds of water is weighed and calculated. Where the water above came from is read out of Genesis. The app will always tell you which one you are getting."},{"id":"weather-2","section":"weather","cat":"tryit","kind":"found","text":"Thunder is the sound of that superheated air blowing outward. Light gets to you almost instantly. Sound crawls, at about one mile every five seconds. So count from the flash to the bang, divide by five, and that is roughly how many miles off the strike was.","more":"Thunder carries about ten miles before the sound gives out. If you can hear it at all, you are close enough to be hit, which is why the safety rule is: when thunder roars, go indoors.","tryit":"From inside the house, count one-Mississippi, two-Mississippi from the next flash until the thunder arrives. Divide by five for miles. Do it twice and you can tell whether the storm is walking toward you or away."},{"id":"weather-3","section":"weather","cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"The biggest hailstone ever measured in the United States fell on Vivian, South Dakota on 23 July 2010. It was 8 inches (20.3 cm) across, 18.62 inches (47.3 cm) around the middle, and weighed 1.94 pounds (0.88 kg). Roughly a soccer ball made of ice.","more":"It sat in a home freezer before the National Weather Service could get to it, and had already shrunk a little by the time it was measured. So the stone that actually landed was bigger than the record it set."},{"id":"weather-4","section":"weather","cat":"weird","kind":"worked","text":"Cut a big hailstone open and you find layers, like an onion. The old explanation, one layer per trip up and down inside the storm, is wrong. Nobody can follow a hailstone around inside a thundercloud. The layers are read as patches of different temperature and wetness it drifted through.","more":"Clear layers form where the stone is collecting water fast enough that it freezes slowly and squeezes the air bubbles out. Cloudy white layers form where it freezes on contact and traps the bubbles. Most of the growing happens in one pass across the updraft, not in loops."},{"id":"weather-5","section":"weather","cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"On 15 January 1885, on a farm in Jericho, Vermont, a nineteen-year-old named Wilson Bentley made the first photograph of a single snow crystal, working outdoors in the cold with a camera bolted to a microscope. It had taken him two winters of failures. He kept going for the rest of his life.","more":"He photographed more than 5,000 crystals. His book Snow Crystals came out in 1931 with 2,453 of the pictures in it, and he died that same year, of pneumonia caught walking home in a snowstorm. Plenty of the snowflake pictures in schoolbooks are still his."},{"id":"weather-6","section":"weather","cat":"weird","kind":"worked","text":"\"No two snowflakes are alike\" has never actually been checked. Nobody has compared them all. What can be said: a large crystal is built from something like a billion billion water molecules, and each of its six arms grows through its own path of cold and damp. Small plain crystals do look identical.","more":"The claim people can actually defend is narrower: two big, complicated crystals matching each other arm for arm would be a staggering coincidence. Two simple six-sided plates matching is ordinary, and has been photographed."},{"id":"weather-7","section":"weather","cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Snow crystals have six sides because ice does. When water freezes, the molecules lock into a repeating six-sided pattern, and the crystal grows outward on that frame. The six arms end up matching each other because they are growing side by side through the same air, not because anything is copying anything."},{"id":"weather-8","section":"weather","cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"The hottest air temperature ever officially recorded is 134 F (56.7 C), at Greenland Ranch in Death Valley, California, on 10 July 1913. It is still the world record. It is also still argued about, because some researchers think the observer misread the thermometer that week.","more":"Death Valley regularly gets past 125 F (52 C) in modern, well-checked instruments, so the place is not in doubt. Only that one reading is."},{"id":"weather-9","section":"weather","cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"The coldest air temperature ever recorded is minus 128.6 F (minus 89.2 C), at the Soviet Vostok station in Antarctica on 21 July 1983. July is midwinter down there. At that temperature a cup of boiling water thrown into the air can turn to ice crystals before it lands."},{"id":"caves-1","section":"caves","cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"On 19 March 1982 a small eruption melted the snow in Mount St. Helens' crater. Close to a billion gallons (4 million cubic metres) of water burst out and tore down the North Fork Toutle River valley, cutting as it went. Nobody watched the Grand Canyon get cut. People watched this one.","more":"Geologists had surveyed marked lines across the channel weeks before. They surveyed the same lines again on 20 March, the morning after. Ten miles (16 km) below the crater the riverbed had dropped an average of 9 ft (2.8 m) overnight. Downstream, four men stood at a gauging station and sampled the flood as it went past. The stuff it cut through was loose volcanic debris dumped by the 1980 eruption, not hard rock, which is a fair part of why it went so fast."},{"id":"caves-10","section":"caves","cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"Movile Cave in Romania was found in 1986 by workers digging on a building site near the Black Sea. It is sealed off from the surface. Fifty-seven animal species have been counted living in it, thirty-seven of them found nowhere else on earth, and most of them have no eyes at all.","more":"Nothing down there runs on sunlight. The bottom of the food chain is bacteria pulling energy out of hydrogen sulfide and methane in the water. That is the rotten-egg gas, and the air in the cave is dangerous to breathe, so scientists go in with tanks and stay under an hour."},{"id":"caves-11","section":"caves","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"On 8 May 1981 the ground opened up in Winter Park, Florida. Over two days the hole widened to 320 feet (98 m) across and 90 feet (27 m) deep. It swallowed a three-bedroom house, a huge sycamore tree, five Porsches from a repair shop, a pickup truck and the town swimming pool.","more":"Florida is built on limestone riddled with holes. This one let go during a stretch of record-low water in the aquifer underneath, when the water that had been helping hold the roof up was gone. The hole is now a lake, and the town keeps it as a park."},{"id":"caves-12","section":"caves","cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Genesis says the fountains of the great deep burst open, the windows of heaven were opened, and water covered the whole earth. Look from the Grand Canyon rim and you see flat stacked layers, and a canyon cut down through them — laid down and then carved, in that order.","more":"Note what is and is not being claimed. The layers are there and can be photographed. The canyon is a mile deep and can be measured. Which event laid the layers and cut the canyon is not something a tape measure can settle, so it is marked here as coming from the record."},{"id":"caves-13","section":"caves","cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"Hang Son Doong in Vietnam holds the largest cave passage ever measured: about 660 feet (200 m) high and 490 feet (150 m) wide, running for more than 3 miles (5 km). A Boeing 747 could fly down the middle of it without its wingtips touching either wall.","more":"Two stretches of its ceiling have collapsed, which lets daylight down into the cave. Trees grow inside, under those holes. On humid days a cloud forms indoors, near the roof, and it rains inside the cave."},{"id":"caves-2","section":"caves","cat":"travel","kind":"record","text":"A Vietnamese man named Ho Khanh found the entrance in 1990, while out in the jungle looking for agarwood to sell. Then he could not find it again. He relocated it in 2008, and in April 2009 a team of British cavers surveyed it and worked out what he had walked into.","more":"The entrance is a hole in a jungle hillside with a cold draught coming out of it, and the roar of an underground river below. It is not obvious from outside that it opens into the biggest passage anyone has measured."},{"id":"caves-3","section":"caves","cat":"build","kind":"worked","text":"Nobody has ever watched a cave form. The explanation is worked out, not observed: rain picks up carbon dioxide from the air and the soil, becomes a weak acid, and eats limestone along cracks. You can check the middle of that argument, though. Water leaving cave springs is carrying dissolved limestone right now.","more":"The step that is genuinely measured is the chemistry. Put limestone in fizzy water and it dissolves. Test water coming out of a cave and it is loaded with calcium it did not have going in. The size of the hole is measured too. It is the story joining the two that is reasoning."},{"id":"caves-4","section":"caves","cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Stalactites hang from the ceiling. Stalagmites stand on the ground. The letters do the remembering: C for ceiling, G for ground. They usually come as a matched pair, because one drip builds both, leaving a smear of mineral on the ceiling on its way past and the rest of it where it lands.","more":"The longest hanging stalactite you can walk up to and look at is about 27 feet (8.2 m) long, in the Jeita Grotto in Lebanon. When a stalactite and the stalagmite under it finally meet, the join is called a column."},{"id":"caves-5","section":"caves","cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"A stalactite starts as a hollow tube a fifth of an inch (4 to 5 mm) across, with water running down the inside. Cavers call those soda straws. When the tube clogs, water starts running down the outside instead, and the fat cone shape begins. Average thickening: 0.005 inches (0.13 mm) a year.","more":"That is the average. A stalactite fed by a fast steady drip can add ten times that, and concrete under a bridge can grow one at 2 mm a day, which is a useful reminder that growth rate depends entirely on the drip, not on the calendar."},{"id":"caves-6","section":"caves","cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"Mammoth Cave in Kentucky is the longest cave known: 426 miles (686 km) of mapped passage, more than any other cave on earth by a wide margin. That figure is not an estimate. Volunteers from the Cave Research Foundation crawled it with tape measures and compasses, survey station by survey station.","more":"Before the Flint Ridge system was connected to it, Mammoth was a 44-mile cave. The extra 380-odd miles came from people going back again and again to check whether a crack was wide enough to squeeze through."},{"id":"caves-7","section":"caves","cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"The deepest known cave is Veryovkina, in the Arabika massif in Georgia. Its floor sits 7,257 feet (2,212 m) below its entrance, which is well over a mile straight down. When it was first written up in 1968, it was thought to be only 377 feet (115 m) deep.","more":"The entrance is a hole about 10 feet by 13 feet in a mountainside. Reaching the bottom takes days, and expeditions have been chased back out by floodwater rising up the shafts."},{"id":"caves-8","section":"caves","cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"The Grand Canyon is measured, not guessed at: 278 river miles (447 km) long, up to 18 miles (29 km) from rim to rim, and about one mile (1.6 km) straight down from rim to river. The South Rim stands near 7,000 feet; the North Rim near 8,000.","more":"The two rims are about 10 miles apart on average, but roughly 215 miles apart by road, because you have to drive all the way around. Hiking rim to rim is about 21 miles and is not a day trip for most people."},{"id":"caves-9","section":"caves","cat":"weird","kind":"worked","text":"How deep the canyon is and how it was cut are two different kinds of statement. The depth is measured with instruments. The cutting was never watched. That account is worked backwards from the shape of the rock, the bends in the river and the gravel it dropped. Geologists still disagree about it.","more":"This is the honest position, and it is not a dodge. A canyon does not come with a label. Everything anyone says about how it got there is reasoning from what is left behind, and reasoning can be revised. The tape measure cannot."},{"id":"volcanoes-1","section":"volcanoes","cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Magma and lava are the same melted rock with different addresses. Underground it is magma. The second it comes out of the vent it is lava. Geologists are strict about this, and it is the cheapest way there is to sound like you know what you are talking about."},{"id":"volcanoes-10","section":"volcanoes","cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"Measured from the sea floor it stands on, the Hawaiian volcano Mauna Kea rises about 30,610 feet (9,330 m). Everest is 29,032 feet (8,849 m) above sea level. Mauna Kea is the taller mountain and loses the title on a technicality, because most of it is underwater.","more":"Only 13,803 feet (4,207 m) of Mauna Kea sticks out above the sea. The rest is a slope you would have to dive to see."},{"id":"volcanoes-11","section":"volcanoes","cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Genesis 7 does not describe the Flood as rain alone. It says the fountains of the great deep were broken up, and then that the windows of heaven were opened. Water out of the ground first, water out of the sky second. That is the order the written account gives.","more":"This is a record, not a reading off an instrument. It is included here because water bursting upward out of the earth is the thing this section is about, and the account puts it first."},{"id":"volcanoes-12","section":"volcanoes","cat":"speed","kind":"found","text":"Films get this backwards. Most lava flows creep along slower than you walk, and people stand next to them taking photographs. The killer is the pyroclastic flow: a ground-hugging cloud of hot ash and gas that comes down the slope faster than 50 mph (80 km/h).","more":"That is what reached Pompeii and Herculaneum. It is also why volcanologists worry less about where lava is going than about whether the eruption column is about to collapse."},{"id":"volcanoes-2","section":"volcanoes","cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"Lava at Kilauea in Hawaii comes out around 2,140 degrees F (1,170 C), and inside a lava tube it can hold near 2,200 degrees F (1,200 C). That is not an estimate. Geologists walk up to the flow and push a metal probe called a thermocouple straight into it.","more":"They also point calibrated thermal cameras at flows they cannot safely reach, then check those readings against the ones taken with the probe."},{"id":"volcanoes-3","section":"volcanoes","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Ol Doinyo Lengai in Tanzania erupts the coolest lava on Earth, about 930 to 1,110 degrees F (500 to 600 C). It is cool enough that it does not glow in daylight. It pours out looking like runny black oil, and rain turns the hardened flows chalk white.","more":"Its lava is made of sodium and potassium carbonates instead of the silicate minerals in ordinary lava. That is why it is so runny and so cool, and why it goes white when water gets at it."},{"id":"volcanoes-4","section":"volcanoes","cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"There are about 1,350 volcanoes on land considered capable of erupting, not counting the near-continuous chain of them running along the sea floor. About 500 of those have actually erupted within written history. On a typical day, roughly 20 are erupting somewhere in the world.","more":"The United States and its territories hold about 170 of them, which is why the USGS runs volcano observatories in Hawaii, Alaska, the Cascades, California and Yellowstone."},{"id":"volcanoes-5","section":"volcanoes","cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"The eruption that buried Pompeii in AD 79 has an eyewitness. Pliny the Younger watched from across the bay and later wrote two letters to the historian Tacitus, describing a cloud shaped like an umbrella pine: a tall trunk with branches spreading at the top.","more":"His description was good enough that volcanologists named that kind of eruption after him. A tall column punching straight up into the sky is still called Plinian."},{"id":"volcanoes-6","section":"volcanoes","cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"On the morning of 27 August 1883 Krakatoa exploded, and people on Rodrigues Island 3,000 miles (4,800 km) away in the Indian Ocean wrote down that they heard the distant roar of heavy guns. In Perth, Australia, 1,930 miles (3,110 km) off, they heard it too.","more":"Barographs, instruments that scratch a line of air pressure onto a paper drum, recorded the pressure wave passing seven times over five days. It went out to the far side of the world and back, out and back, until it faded. Nobody at those weather stations knew yet what had happened."},{"id":"volcanoes-7","section":"volcanoes","cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"Tambora in Indonesia erupted in April 1815 and blew about 4,200 feet off its own summit. The next year, farmers in New England wrote down frost in every single month and a snowstorm in June. They called 1816 the year without a summer, and also Eighteen Hundred and Froze to Death.","more":"On 5 June 1816 the temperature in Ipswich, Massachusetts hit 92 degrees F. By the next morning it had fallen to 43. Crops failed across the northeast and in Europe, and people who kept diaries recorded all of it as it happened."},{"id":"volcanoes-8","section":"volcanoes","cat":"build","kind":"worked","text":"Nobody has ever looked into a magma chamber. Their size and depth are worked out, not seen: earthquake waves slow down when they pass through melted rock, and the ground above swells by a few inches before an eruption. Instruments read both. Eyes read neither.","more":"The tilt is far too small to notice standing on it. It is measured with tiltmeters and with satellites that compare radar images of the same hillside taken weeks apart."},{"id":"volcanoes-9","section":"volcanoes","cat":"speed","kind":"found","text":"Old Faithful in Yellowstone throws boiling water 106 to 184 feet (30 to 55 m) into the air about every 90 minutes. Rangers predict it by timing the eruption before: the longer it plays, the longer the wait for the next one. Each eruption empties 3,700 to 8,400 gallons.","more":"It is neither the biggest geyser in the park nor the most punctual. It is simply the big one that keeps to a schedule people can plan a day around."},{"id":"rocks-1","section":"rocks","cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"A mineral's hardness is really just a list of what it can scratch. Talc, number 1, gives way to a fingernail. Quartz, number 7, cuts glass. Diamond sits at 10, and the only thing that reliably scratches a diamond is another diamond.","more":"The ten steps in order are talc, gypsum, calcite, fluorite, apatite, orthoclase, quartz, topaz, corundum, diamond. Nothing on the list scratches anything above it."},{"id":"rocks-10","section":"rocks","cat":"travel","kind":"found","text":"Pumice is rock full of frozen gas bubbles, and it floats. After some eruptions, whole rafts of floating pumice drift away across the open sea and get tracked by ships and satellites for months before they finally waterlog and sink.","more":"Drop a piece in a glass and it bobs. Leave it long enough and water works into the holes and it goes under, which is how the rafts eventually break up."},{"id":"rocks-11","section":"rocks","cat":"senses","kind":"found","text":"Granite is not one substance. Look closely at a polished granite counter and the speckles are separate minerals grown together: glassy gray quartz, blocky pink or white feldspar, and black flecks of mica that peel off in shiny sheets. You are looking at a mixture, not a material.","more":"Get your eye close to a granite countertop, step or gravestone and you can usually pick out three or four different kinds of grain without any magnifier at all."},{"id":"rocks-12","section":"rocks","cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Genesis names minerals early. Describing the land of Havilah in chapter 2, it lists gold, bdellium and onyx stone. Onyx is banded quartz, and people still cut and polish it."},{"id":"rocks-2","section":"rocks","cat":"tryit","kind":"found","text":"Your fingernail is about 2.5 on the hardness scale and a copper coin is about 3. A rock your nail marks is softer than calcite. A rock that shrugs off a steel key is harder than about 5.5. That is the whole test geologists carry into the field.","tryit":"Pick up a rock outside. Drag a fingernail across it, then the edge of a copper coin, then a steel key. Whichever leaves a scratch tells you roughly how hard the rock is."},{"id":"rocks-3","section":"rocks","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"The hardness scale looks like a ruler and is not one. Corundum is 9 and diamond is 10, one step apart. But pressed with a hard point and measured properly, diamond resists about five times better than corundum. The numbers are an order of finish, not a measurement.","more":"On the Vickers test corundum comes in near 2,035 and diamond near 10,000. The gaps between the ten steps are all different sizes, and the last one is enormous."},{"id":"rocks-4","section":"rocks","cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"The scale is named for Friedrich Mohs, a German mineral expert who in 1812 lined ten minerals up in the order of which scratched which. He never measured a force or built a machine. He just made a ranking, and it was useful enough that it is still in every geology kit.","more":"This is the history of the science rather than a measurement: the scale exists because one man sorted a drawer of samples and wrote the order down."},{"id":"rocks-5","section":"rocks","cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"Miners at Naica in Mexico broke into a chamber packed with clear gypsum beams. The largest measure about 37 feet (11 m) long and 13 feet (4 m) thick, and weigh roughly 55 tons. They are single crystals, longer than a school bus.","more":"The chamber sits near 136 degrees F (58 C) with the air almost completely saturated with water, so people could only stay a few minutes at a time even in cooled suits. When the mine's pumps were switched off in October 2015 the water came back and covered the crystals again."},{"id":"rocks-6","section":"rocks","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Ruby and sapphire are the same mineral. Both are corundum, aluminum and oxygen. A trace of chromium turns it red and it gets called ruby. Traces of iron and titanium turn it blue and it gets called sapphire. Same hardness, same recipe, different pinch of impurity, wildly different price."},{"id":"rocks-7","section":"rocks","cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Obsidian is glass a volcano made. Lava cooled so fast the minerals never got to line up into crystals. With no grain to follow, a flake of obsidian can come to an edge a few nanometers across, thinner than the edge of a steel razor. People chipped it into knives and arrowheads.","more":"A steel scalpel edge is blunt by comparison, which is why obsidian blades have been used in some surgery and in laboratory work where a very clean cut matters."},{"id":"rocks-8","section":"rocks","cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Weigh the rock of Earth's crust and 46 percent of it is oxygen, the same element you are breathing, locked into minerals instead of floating loose. Silicon is another 28 percent. Most rock is those two joined together, with some aluminum and iron mixed in.","more":"Aluminum comes third at about 8 percent. Between them, oxygen, silicon and aluminum account for more than four fifths of everything you walk on."},{"id":"rocks-9","section":"rocks","cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"Table salt is a mineral. Its proper name is halite, and it is the one rock people eat on purpose. Halite always breaks along the same three directions, so the grains come apart into little cubes. Tip some onto dark paper and look closely and the cubes are right there.","more":"Halite is soft, only about 2 or 2.5 on the hardness scale, which is why salt crushes under a spoon and quartz does not."},{"id":"sky-1","section":"sky","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"The moon does spin. It just spins at exactly the rate it travels round us, one turn per orbit, about 27 days, so the same face stays pointed at earth the whole time. If the moon stopped spinning tomorrow, we would start seeing every side of it.","more":"Its orbit is slightly lopsided and slightly tilted, so from here the moon appears to rock and nod a little. Over a month that rocking lets us peek round the edges, and about 59 percent of the surface has now been seen from the ground.","tryit":"Find the moon in daylight. Around first quarter, when it is half lit, look up in the afternoon. It is high in the southern sky and pale blue-white against the blue. The moon is up in daytime about as often as at night. It just loses to the sun."},{"id":"sky-10","section":"sky","cat":"senses","kind":"worked","text":"Auroras are not reflections off polar ice, which is the old guess. Particles streaming off the sun get steered by earth's magnetic field down toward the poles, crash into oxygen and nitrogen high in the atmosphere and make them glow. It is a neon sign, using the whole sky as the tube.","more":"Nobody can see the particles, so the cause is worked out rather than watched, from satellites that measure the solar wind before it arrives and from lab tests of which gas glows what colour. Oxygen gives green from roughly 60 to 120 miles (100 to 200 km) up and red above that. The southern version, the aurora australis, often lights up at the same moment, in a matching shape."},{"id":"sky-11","section":"sky","cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"Most shooting stars are made by something between a grain of sand and a pea. It is moving so fast that the air piling up in front of it is crushed and heated until the grain glows white and is gone. About 48 tons of that dust hits the atmosphere every single day.","more":"The grain is not burning up from rubbing against the air, which is the usual explanation and is wrong. It is the air itself getting compressed and superheated in front of it, the same way a bicycle pump gets hot."},{"id":"sky-12","section":"sky","cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Genesis day four: sun, moon and stars set in place for signs and for seasons, and for days and years. Read that way, the sky is a calendar. The spin that makes a day, the moon's cycle that makes a month, the 23.4-degree tilt that makes a year turn.","more":"The pieces of that calendar are all measurable and all measured: 23 hours 56 minutes for a turn, about 29 and a half days from one full moon to the next, 23.4 degrees of tilt. What the account adds is that they were put there on purpose, to be read."},{"id":"sky-2","section":"sky","cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"Nobody saw the far side of the moon until October 1959, when a Soviet probe called Luna 3 flew past and sent back photographs. Calling it the dark side is wrong: it gets as much sunlight as the side we look at. It is the hidden side, not the dark one.","more":"The pictures were grainy and there were only a handful of them, but they showed something nobody expected: the far side is covered in craters and almost lacks the big dark smooth plains that make the face we see look like a face."},{"id":"sky-3","section":"sky","cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"Tides are measured with tide gauges, every day, everywhere. The biggest are in the Bay of Fundy in Canada, where the difference between low and high water averages about 52 feet (16 m). That is a four-storey building. The worldwide average is closer to 3 feet (1 m).","more":"Roughly 110 billion tons (100 billion tonnes) of water moves in and out of that bay on a single tide, which is more than every river on earth is carrying put together at that moment. Boats in Fundy harbours sit flat on mud twice a day."},{"id":"sky-4","section":"sky","cat":"build","kind":"worked","text":"Most coasts get two high tides a day, not one, and the reason is reasoned out rather than watched. The moon's pull heaps the ocean up on the side facing it, and also on the far side, where the water is pulled least and gets left behind. Earth turns through both bulges daily.","more":"The sun does the same thing, with about half the pull. When sun and moon line up you get the biggest tides of the month, called spring tides, which has nothing to do with the season. When they pull at right angles you get the smallest, called neap tides."},{"id":"sky-5","section":"sky","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Seasons have nothing to do with how far away the sun is. Earth is actually closest to the sun in early January, in the dead middle of northern winter. What makes summer is tilt: earth's axis leans over by 23.4 degrees, so for half the year your side leans sunward.","more":"The gap between nearest and farthest is only about three percent, which is far too small to matter. And there is a simpler killer for the distance idea: Australia has summer while Canada has winter, on the same planet, at the same distance, on the same day. Leaning sunward means the light hits at a steeper angle and is squeezed onto less ground, plus the days are longer."},{"id":"sky-6","section":"sky","cat":"tryit","kind":"found","text":"Your shadow is the hand of a clock. It swings around the ground all day because the earth is turning underneath you, at about 1,000 mph (1,600 km/h) if you are near the equator. You do not feel any of it, for the same reason you cannot feel the speed of a smooth aeroplane.","more":"The shadow also changes length through the year. Shortest at midday in summer, when the sun is high, longest at midday in winter, when it is low. That change is the tilt, showing up on your driveway.","tryit":"Stand somewhere sunny and have someone put a stone at the tip of your shadow. Go away for fifteen minutes. Come back, stand in exactly the same footprints, and put down a second stone. The gap between the stones is the earth turning."},{"id":"sky-7","section":"sky","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Earth turns once in 23 hours 56 minutes and 4 seconds, measured against the stars. The day is 24 hours because we time it by the sun instead, and earth has slid along its orbit in the meantime, so it has to turn a little extra to face the sun again.","more":"Those four missing minutes a day add up. It is why the night sky in January is not the night sky in July: the stars come back around four minutes earlier each night, which is two hours a month."},{"id":"sky-8","section":"sky","cat":"speed","kind":"found","text":"Sunlight takes about 8 minutes and 20 seconds to get here, crossing roughly 93 million miles (150 million km). So the sun you can see out of the window is the sun as it was a few minutes ago. Everything in the sky is a picture of the past, just by a little.","more":"Light from the moon takes about 1.3 seconds, which is why radio conversations with Apollo crews had that awkward pause in them. Light from the nearest star after the sun takes over four years."},{"id":"sky-9","section":"sky","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"The sun is about 400 times wider than the moon, and it happens to sit about 400 times further away. Those two numbers cancel out, which is why the sun and the moon look almost exactly the same size in our sky, and why the moon can cover the sun during a total eclipse.","more":"No other planet with moons gets a fit that neat. It is also not permanent: the moon drifts about an inch and a half further from earth each year, and eventually it will be too small in the sky to cover the sun completely."},{"id":"quakes-1","section":"quakes","cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"Each whole step up the magnitude scale means the ground shakes ten times as far, and about 32 times as much energy comes out. So a magnitude 7 is not a bit worse than a 6. It would take roughly 32 sixes to add up to one seven.","more":"Two steps, from 6 to 8, is a hundred times the shaking and about a thousand times the energy. The scale is written that way because otherwise the numbers would run into the millions."},{"id":"quakes-10","section":"quakes","cat":"tryit","kind":"found","text":"One earthquake sends out two kinds of wave from the same spot at the same moment. The push-pull P wave travels fastest through rock and arrives first. The sideways S wave lags behind and does more damage. Measure the gap between them and you know how far away the quake was.","tryit":"Press your ear flat on a wooden table and have someone tap the far end. You hear the tap through the wood before you hear it through the air. Same idea: how fast a shake travels depends entirely on what it is traveling through."},{"id":"quakes-11","section":"quakes","cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Some buildings do not sit on the ground. They sit on thick pads of rubber layered with steel and lead. When the ground jerks sideways, the pads squash and shear and the building above lags behind, moving far less than the dirt under it.","more":"Hospitals, museums and emergency centers in Japan, New Zealand and California are built this way, and instruments inside them have recorded real earthquakes doing exactly what the design predicted."},{"id":"quakes-12","section":"quakes","cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Day three in Genesis is land moving on a scale nobody has ever watched. The waters under the sky are gathered into one place, and dry land appears. The account gives an order, water first and then land standing out of it, and calls the dry part Earth.","more":"Genesis names the dry land on day three, gathered out from under the water. That order — water first, then land standing out of it — is worth holding onto when you look at a cliff face made of flat layers that were laid down wet."},{"id":"quakes-2","section":"quakes","cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"Charles Richter built his scale in 1935, for medium earthquakes in southern California, and the name stuck to everything after it. Big quakes broke it: above about magnitude 7 it flattens out and stops telling them apart. The numbers you hear today come from the moment magnitude scale instead.","more":"News reports still say Richter out of habit. The two scales are set up to agree in the middle of the range, so a modern magnitude 5 means about what Richter's magnitude 5 meant."},{"id":"quakes-3","section":"quakes","cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"The strongest earthquake ever measured struck southern Chile near Valdivia on 22 May 1960: magnitude 9.5. The shaking lasted about ten minutes. Hundreds of miles of coastline tore at once, and the tsunami it launched was still killing people in Hawaii and Japan a day later.","more":"In Hilo, Hawaii, the wave arrived about fifteen hours after the quake and killed 61 people. Japan is a quarter of the way around the world from Chile, and it got hit the following day."},{"id":"quakes-4","section":"quakes","cat":"speed","kind":"found","text":"In deep water a tsunami crosses the ocean at over 500 mph, about the speed of an airliner, while lifting the surface only a few feet. A ship sitting right on top of one feels nothing unusual. It only piles up into something terrible when it reaches shallow water.","more":"Buoys anchored in the deep ocean catch the tiny change in sea level as the wave goes under them and radio it to warning centers, which is how a coast thousands of miles away gets hours of notice."},{"id":"quakes-5","section":"quakes","cat":"senses","kind":"found","text":"A tsunami announces itself. Sometimes the sea drains away first, stranding fish; sometimes it comes in and in and does not go out, frothing oddly. In 2004 a ten-year-old named Tilly Smith recognized the wrong-looking water at Mai Khao Beach in Thailand from a geography lesson two weeks earlier.","more":"She told her parents, her father told a hotel security guard, and the beach was cleared onto the second floor of a hotel before the wave arrived. Mai Khao was one of very few beaches on Phuket with no one killed."},{"id":"quakes-6","section":"quakes","cat":"speed","kind":"found","text":"The plates of Earth's surface drift about as fast as your fingernails grow, roughly one to four inches a year. That part is measured, not reasoned: GPS receivers bolted to bedrock all over the world report their own positions, and the positions keep changing by exactly those small amounts.","more":"The Pacific plate is one of the quicker ones, moving something like 3 to 4 inches a year. The North American plate ambles along at closer to one inch."},{"id":"quakes-7","section":"quakes","cat":"build","kind":"worked","text":"Nobody has ever been below the crust, and nobody has seen the core. What is down there is worked out from the way earthquake waves bend, slow and stop. Sideways shear waves cannot travel through liquid, and they vanish partway down, so the outer core is reasoned to be molten.","more":"In 1936 the Danish seismologist Inge Lehmann noticed waves arriving in places a completely liquid core should have left silent, and argued there was a solid inner core inside the liquid one. That is still how it is done. No camera, no sample, just wiggles on a chart."},{"id":"quakes-8","section":"quakes","cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"The deepest hole anyone has drilled is the Kola borehole in Russia, finished in 1989 at 40,230 feet (12,262 m). That is 7.6 miles straight down. The rock at the bottom was around 356 degrees F (180 C), far hotter than planned, and the drilling stopped.","more":"It never came close to leaving the crust. How much further the crust goes is not something anyone has dug to find out; it is worked out from earthquake waves."},{"id":"quakes-9","section":"quakes","cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"Big earthquakes are rarer than the news makes them feel. Averaged across the years 2000 to 2021, the world got about 135 quakes of magnitude 6 a year, around 13 of magnitude 7, and roughly one of magnitude 8 or stronger. Small ones happen constantly, everywhere.","more":"Thousands of quakes too small to feel are recorded every single day. Most of what a seismograph draws is a planet quietly fidgeting."}];
